## Tailnet Model

- Policy model: Tailscale `{{ tailnet.policy_model }}`
- MagicDNS: {{ 'enabled' if tailnet.magicdns else 'disabled' }}
- Split DNS: `{{ tailnet.split_dns_suffixes | join('` and `') }}` resolve through home DNS
- Advertised subnet routes: `{{ tailnet.advertised_routes | join('` and `') }}`
- Subnet router host: `{{ tailnet.subnet_router.host }}` (`{{ tailnet.subnet_router.address }}`)
- Exit node use: none

The tailnet does not export `lan`, `iot`, `guest`, `tv`, or `work`. Remote access is for administration and a small app set, not general browsing across the house.

## Split DNS Behaviour

- Tailnet devices keep their normal internet DNS path.
- Requests for `{{ dns.split_dns_suffixes[0] }}` go to AdGuard Home only.
- Requests for `{{ dns.split_dns_suffixes[1] }}` also go to AdGuard Home only.
- Guest VLAN clients never receive internal `{{ dns.zone }}` answers.
- Work VLAN clients use public recursion.
- Edge servers that join the tailnet use MagicDNS for node identity and can still resolve `{{ dns.zone }}` over split DNS when they need homelab service names.

This avoids dragging all DNS through home when all that is really needed is internal name resolution.

## Private Tailnet Naming

If an edge server joins the tailnet, it already has a private Tailscale name. That is the right place to start.

- Use MagicDNS for node-to-node administration.
- Keep split DNS for `{{ dns.zone }}` so tailnet devices can reach named homelab services.
- Use `{{ dns.private_tailnet_domain.zone }}` when you want private service names on your own domain instead of `{{ tailnet.private_naming.tailnet_native_namespace }}`.
  That gives the tailnet private DNS without confusing it with the public `{{ dns.public_domain.zone }}` path.

Preferred private names:

{% for record in dns.local_records if record.fqdn.endswith(dns.private_tailnet_domain.zone) -%}
- `{{ record.fqdn }}`
{% endfor %}
Those names are private because Tailscale split DNS resolves them to private tailnet or homelab endpoints, and Tailscale grants decide who can connect.

## Public DNS To Tailnet IPs

This design does not publish public DNS records that point at Tailscale IPs.

Dangers:

- The hostnames become publicly enumerable even if the services are not reachable.
- Public TLS certificates for those names put them in Certificate Transparency logs.
- DNS caches and split-horizon mistakes become harder to reason about.
- Private `{{ dns.public_domain.zone }}` names collide with public naming and create avoidable confusion.

Rule:

- Public `*.{{ dns.public_domain.zone }}` records point to public ingress.
- Private `*.{{ dns.private_tailnet_domain.zone }}` records resolve only over split DNS to tailnet users.

## Tailnet Access Model

Four access levels exist:

{% for identity in tailnet.identities -%}
- `{{ identity.id }}`: {{ identity.purpose }}
{% endfor %}
Admin-only remote service:

{% for app in tailnet.approved_apps if app.allowed_identities == ['group:homelab-admins'] -%}
- `{{ app.fqdn }}:{{ app.ports | join(', ') | replace('tcp/', '') }}`
{% endfor %}
Approved remote app set for `homelab-app-users`:

{% for app in tailnet.approved_apps if 'group:homelab-app-users' in app.allowed_identities -%}
- `{{ app.fqdn }}:{{ app.ports | join(', ') | replace('tcp/', '') }}`
{% endfor %}
Remote storage path:

- `{{ tailnet.storage_access.host }}:445` for `SMB` over the tailnet
- `nas.{{ dns.zone }}:443` for NAS administration
- `NFS` stays internal

Remote NAS storage uses `SMB` over Tailscale for managed laptops and desktops, plus HTTPS for NAS administration.

`WebDAV`, `Taildrive`, and `NFS` are not used.

## SSH Model

SSH access is simple:

- Linux edge and server nodes advertise Tailscale SSH.
- Tailnet policy controls who can SSH and as which users.
- High-risk users such as `{{ tailnet.ssh.high_risk_users.users | join('`, `') }}` use `{{ tailnet.ssh.high_risk_users.action }}` mode.
- Password SSH is disabled.
- Public port `22` is closed.

OpenSSH keys still matter for a few cases:

- break-glass recovery
- bootstrap before Tailscale is online
- deploy keys and service accounts
- platforms where Tailscale SSH is not the right fit

Tailscale SSH is the admin path for those nodes.

## `{{ tailnet.edge_publishing.public_domain }}` Publishing Model

`{{ tailnet.edge_publishing.public_domain }}` is for selected HTTP(S) apps only. It is not a shortcut to make the whole homelab public.

- Public DNS for `*.{{ tailnet.edge_publishing.public_domain }}` points to `{{ tailnet.edge_publishing.edge_node }}`.
- Edge servers join the tailnet and use it for private administration.
- The edge proxy reaches home backends over Tailscale.
- TLS for `{{ tailnet.edge_publishing.public_domain }}` terminates on the edge proxy with ordinary public certificates.
- Tailscale protects the edge-to-home transport. It does not authenticate the browser session to `{{ tailnet.edge_publishing.public_domain }}`.
- Put an auth layer on the edge proxy for anything that is not meant to be publicly reachable.
- Do not publish NAS file protocols on `{{ tailnet.edge_publishing.public_domain }}`.

Published apps:

{% for app in tailnet.edge_publishing.published_apps -%}
- `{{ app.public_name }}` -> `{{ app.upstream }}`
{% endfor %}
Tailscale Funnel and Tailscale-issued HTTPS certificates are tied to the tailnet `{{ tailnet.private_naming.tailnet_native_namespace }}` namespace, not `{{ tailnet.edge_publishing.public_domain }}`.

## Tailnet-Only `{{ tailnet.edge_publishing.public_domain }}` Names

If you want private app URLs on your own domain, use `{{ dns.private_tailnet_domain.zone }}`, not the same names you use publicly.

Examples:

{% for record in dns.local_records if record.fqdn.endswith(dns.private_tailnet_domain.zone) -%}
- `{{ record.fqdn }}` for private {{ record.fqdn.split('.')[0] | replace('-', ' ') }} access
{% endfor %}
This gives you what you actually want:

- your own domain in the browser
- access gated by Tailscale grants
- no dependency on `{{ tailnet.private_naming.tailnet_native_namespace }}` names for day-to-day use

It does not replace app auth. Tailscale grants decide who can open the TCP connection. Sensitive apps keep their own login.

## Funnel And Services

This design does not use Tailscale Services or Tailscale Funnel.

- Private app names use `{{ dns.private_tailnet_domain.zone }}`.
- Public app names use `{{ dns.public_domain.zone }}`.
- `{{ tailnet.private_naming.tailnet_native_namespace }}` names remain available for raw device identity and administration.

## Grants Structure

```json
{
  "groups": {
{% set groups = tailnet.identities | selectattr('type', 'equalto', 'group') | list %}
{% for identity in groups %}
    "{{ identity.id }}": []{{ "," if not loop.last else "" }}
{% endfor %}
  },
  "tagOwners": {
{% set tag_items = tailnet.tag_owners | dictsort %}
{% for tag, owners in tag_items %}
    "{{ tag }}": {{ owners | tojson }}{{ "," if not loop.last else "" }}
{% endfor %}
  },
  "hosts": {
    "meerkat-mgmt": "{{ (zones | selectattr('id', 'equalto', 'management') | first).cidr }}",
    "meerkat-server": "{{ (zones | selectattr('id', 'equalto', 'server') | first).cidr }}",
{% set app_hosts = tailnet.approved_apps | rejectattr('service', 'equalto', 'adguard-admin') | list %}
{% for app in app_hosts %}
{% set short = app.fqdn.split('.')[0] %}
    "{{ short }}": "{{ app.address }}"{{ "," if not loop.last else "" }}
{% endfor %}
  },
  "grants": [
    {
      "src": ["group:homelab-admins"],
      "dst": ["meerkat-mgmt", "meerkat-server"],
      "ip": ["*"]
    },
    {
      "src": ["group:homelab-app-users"],
      "dst": ["ha", "proxy"],
      "ip": ["tcp:443", "tcp:8123"]
    },
    {
      "src": ["group:homelab-storage-users"],
      "dst": ["nas"],
      "ip": ["tcp:443", "tcp:445"]
    },
    {
      "src": ["tag:edge-proxy"],
      "dst": ["ha", "proxy"],
      "ip": ["tcp:443", "tcp:8123"]
    }
  ]
}
```

This is the policy structure used by the design.

